CAT = "phrBusiness"; SEG = "BUS"
# Functional Area 01, Business Management (14%): environment, stakeholders, risk, metrics, culture.
Q = [
("A plant started the year with 210 employees and ended with 190. There were 30 separations during the year. What is the annual turnover rate?",
 "15%", ["14.3%", "15.8%", "10%"],
 "Turnover is separations divided by average headcount: 30 / ((210 + 190) / 2) = 30 / 200 = 15%. Dividing by the starting headcount gives 14.3%, and by the ending headcount 15.8%. 10% is the net fall in headcount (20 / 200), not separations."),
("An HR manager wants to see how long it takes from approving a requisition to a candidate accepting the offer. Which metric should she track?",
 "Time-to-fill", ["Time-to-start", "Cost-per-hire", "Offer acceptance rate"],
 "Time-to-fill runs from requisition approval (or posting) to offer acceptance. Time-to-start runs to the first day of work, and cost-per-hire measures spending, not time."),
("A leadership course cost $40,000 and produced measurable savings of $100,000 in its first year. What is the return on investment?",
 "150%", ["250%", "60%", "40%"],
 "ROI is net benefit divided by cost: ($100,000 - $40,000) / $40,000 = 150%. Dividing total benefit by cost gives 250%, a benefit-cost ratio rather than ROI."),
("In a SWOT analysis, a competitor opening a plant in the same town is best classed as:",
 "A threat", ["A weakness", "An opportunity", "A strength"],
 "Threats and opportunities are external; strengths and weaknesses are internal. A new competitor for the same local labor pool is an external threat."),
("A PESTLE scan asks HR to consider a new state paid leave law. Which letter does that fall under?",
 "Legal", ["Political", "Economic", "Social"],
 "PESTLE covers political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors. A law that has passed is a legal factor; the debate before it passed would be political."),
("A company has managers who each supervise three or four people across eight layers. This structure is best described as:",
 "Tall with a narrow span of control", ["Flat with a wide span of control at each level", "A matrix with dual reporting", "A divisional structure by region"],
 "Many layers with few direct reports per manager is a tall structure with a narrow span of control, which slows decisions but allows close supervision."),
("Engineers on a product team report to a project leader and to their functional engineering manager. This is:",
 "A matrix structure", ["A functional structure", "A divisional structure", "A flat structure"],
 "A matrix structure gives employees two reporting lines, one functional and one project or product. It improves coordination but can blur authority."),
("HR consolidates payroll, benefits enrollment and leave administration for all divisions into one team. This model is called:",
 "Shared services", ["A center of excellence", "An HR business partner model", "Outsourcing"],
 "A shared services center handles routine transactions for the whole organization. A center of excellence holds specialist expertise, such as compensation design."),
("Which HR role sits with a business unit's leaders and translates their goals into people plans?",
 "HR business partner", ["Shared services analyst", "HRIS administrator", "Benefits specialist"],
 "An HR business partner works alongside unit leaders as a consultant and draws on centers of excellence and shared services for delivery."),
("In Lewin's change model, communicating why the current way of working must end happens in which stage?",
 "Unfreezing", ["Changing", "Refreezing", "Transitioning"],
 "Unfreezing creates readiness by showing the need for change. Changing is the move itself, and refreezing anchors the new behavior in policy and habit."),
("Kotter's model of leading change begins with which step?",
 "Create a sense of urgency", ["Form a guiding coalition of leaders", "Communicate the vision", "Generate short-term wins"],
 "Kotter's eight steps start with urgency, then a guiding coalition and a vision. Short-term wins come later, after people have been enabled to act."),
("Before rolling out a new performance system, HR compares the skills managers have with the skills the system needs. This is:",
 "A gap analysis", ["A stay interview", "A job evaluation", "A benchmarking survey"],
 "A gap analysis compares the current state with the desired state to show what training, staffing or process changes are needed."),
("An HR generalist finds that her brother's firm has bid on the company's staffing contract. What should she do first?",
 "Disclose it and step away", ["Review the bid herself", "Ask her brother to lower the bid", "Wait until the contract is signed"],
 "A conflict of interest should be disclosed promptly and the person should withdraw from the decision, as most codes of conduct require."),
("An internal I-9 audit finds a form with Section 2 left blank. What is the right fix?",
 "Complete it now with today's date", ["Backdate it to the employee's hire date", "Destroy it and start again", "Leave it until an inspection"],
 "USCIS guidance is to correct or complete the form with the current date and initials, never to backdate. A note explaining the correction is good practice."),
("A company's leaders want to judge whether its hiring brings in people from a wide range of backgrounds. Which data should HR report?",
 "Applicant flow by stage", ["Average tenure by department", "Overtime hours by shift", "Training hours per employee"],
 "Applicant flow data shows who applies, who is interviewed and who is hired at each stage, which reveals where candidates drop out."),
("A firm's employer brand is mainly shaped by:",
 "What employees say about working there", ["The company's logo and colors", "The size of its yearly benefits budget", "Its share price on the market"],
 "Employer brand is the reputation the organization has as a place to work, built largely from employee experience and what people say about it."),
("A manager asks HR how to show the company's core values in day-to-day decisions. Which step would help most?",
 "Tie values to performance reviews", ["Print the values on posters", "Mention them at each annual company meeting", "Add them to the job ads only"],
 "Values take hold when they shape what is measured and rewarded. Including value-based behaviors in reviews makes them part of everyday decisions."),
("A whistleblower hotline is mainly a control for which kind of risk?",
 "Undetected misconduct", ["Market price changes", "Natural disasters", "Currency fluctuations"],
 "Anonymous reporting channels help organizations find fraud, harassment and other misconduct early, and many ethics programs require them."),
("Which metric shows the share of new hires who leave within their first year?",
 "First-year turnover", ["Absence rate", "Yield ratio", "New hire offer acceptance rate"],
 "First-year (new hire) turnover reveals problems with selection, onboarding or early management that overall turnover can hide."),
("A business plans to replace a manual invoice process with software. What should HR plan for first?",
 "Retraining or redeploying staff", ["Cutting all clerical jobs at once", "Raising pay for every clerk", "Keeping the change confidential until launch"],
 "Automation changes jobs. HR's role is to plan the people side: which roles change, what training is needed and whether redeployment or a RIF follows."),
("A company had 150 employees and 2,250 scheduled workdays last month, and 90 days were lost to unscheduled absence. What is the absence rate?",
 "4%", ["6%", "0.6%", "2.5%"],
 "Absence rate is days lost divided by scheduled days: 90 / 2,250 = 4%. Dividing days lost by headcount gives 0.6 days per person, not a rate."),
("A firm compares its benefit costs with those of similar employers in its industry. This is called:",
 "Benchmarking", ["Job analysis", "Forecasting", "Auditing"],
 "Benchmarking compares the organization's practices or results with peers to judge whether they are competitive and to find better practices."),
("An HR director must explain a rise in turnover to the executive team. Which presentation is most useful?",
 "Trends by unit with likely causes", ["A list of every employee who left", "The full text of exit interviews", "One overall percentage only"],
 "Executives need a trend, where it is concentrated and what drives it, so they can decide what to do. Raw lists and transcripts hide the pattern."),
("Which practice best supports continuous improvement in an HR department?",
 "Review metrics and adjust processes", ["Keep processes fixed for years", "Change processes without data", "Measure results only at yearly audits"],
 "Continuous improvement means measuring results regularly, finding causes of problems and adjusting processes, as in a plan-do-check-act cycle."),
("A company's insider threat program flags an employee downloading large files before resigning. Which HR policy most directly supports action here?",
 "An acceptable use policy", ["A dress and appearance policy", "A tuition policy", "A travel policy"],
 "An acceptable use or information security policy sets the rules on company data and systems, giving grounds to investigate and act on misuse."),
("Which statement best describes an organization's mission?",
 "What it does and for whom", ["Where it hopes to be in ten years", "The rules for employee conduct", "Its financial targets for the year"],
 "A mission states the organization's purpose today. A vision describes the future it is aiming for, and values describe how it behaves on the way."),
("Before HR proposes a new wellness program, the CFO asks for the expected cost per employee per year. Which business skill is HR using?",
 "Building a business case", ["Running a focus group", "Writing a job description", "Conducting a job analysis"],
 "A business case sets out costs, expected benefits and risks so leaders can compare the proposal with other uses of the money."),
("A recruiter reaches 300 applicants, interviews 60 and hires 12. What is the interview-to-hire yield ratio?",
 "20%", ["4%", "25%", "5%"],
 "Yield ratio is the share of candidates moving from one stage to the next: 12 hires / 60 interviews = 20%. Hires divided by all applicants gives 4%."),
]
